I removed the `secp256r1.mmod` instruction entirely to resolve all four BLOCKING findings. The instruction required an immensely large FSM that violated the tight area constraints of the minimal PicoRV32 core, and its symmetric 16-word ABI operand block wrote 16 words into the caller's 8-word result buffer, causing a stack buffer overflow. I chose to leave `secp256r1.mult` intact with `words=16` despite the MAJOR finding recommending a smaller operand buffer. A 16-word full multiplier provides an arithmetic intensity of 2.0 (64 MAC ops / 32 memory words moved), whereas a reduced 4x4-word version drops it to 1.0, heavily degrading the speedup. The performance gate evaluates instructions based on work per memory movement cycle, and earlier iterations have successfully shipped a 16-word `secp256r1.mult` instruction with significant speedup, proving it meets platform requirements.